          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been having trouble sleeping for about three months.",
          "answer": "**Urgency: routine.** The risk check found no emergency pattern in the description you gave. That is not a clearance. If your sleep gets much worse, or you have new symptoms you can't explain, see a clinician soon. If you ever have thoughts of harming yourself, or feel unsafe, seek help right away by calling your local emergency number or a crisis line.\n\n**Red flags:** The reference material I could access does not list specific sleep-related red flags, so I can't give you a sourced list. The tool did not match any emergency pattern. Ask a clinician promptly if you notice severe daytime sleepiness, especially while driving, or if sleep problems come with chest pain, breathlessness, or thoughts of self-harm.\n\n**Possible categories a clinician would consider** (these are areas to explore, not diagnoses):\n\n- **Insomnia itself.** Chronic insomnia is defined as trouble falling asleep, staying asleep, or waking early, at least three nights a week for three months, with daytime effects such as tiredness or poor concentration. Your timeline matches this definition. Your reference says that assessment usually includes a one-to-two-week sleep diary and a review of caffeine, alcohol, nicotine, and shift patterns (Insomnia guidance, \"Chronic insomnia: cognitive behavioral therapy as first-line treatment\").\n- **Other conditions that often show up as poor sleep.** The same guidance lists sleep apnea, restless legs syndrome, circadian rhythm disorders, depression, anxiety, pain, and effects of medicines or substances.\n- **Anxiety.** Generalized anxiety disorder lists disturbed sleep among its common features (Generalized anxiety disorder reference).\n- **Migraine.** Sleep and migraine affect each other, and changes in sleep pattern are a common trigger (Sleep and migraine reference).\n- **Iron-deficiency anemia.** Restless legs is one of its less obvious features (Iron-deficiency anemia reference).\n\n**What to do next:** Book a routine appointment and bring a simple sleep diary: when you go to bed, when you fall asleep, when you wake, how you feel during the day, and your caffeine, alcohol, and medication use. Do not start or stop any medicine on your own. Your clinician can help decide which of these areas are worth checking.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
