          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is medication the usual answer?",
          "answer": "Medication is not the usual answer in every case. Whether it is used, and how much weight it gets next to lifestyle changes, depends on the condition.\n\nThe reference material I searched shows a common pattern. Lifestyle measures are usually the foundation, and medication is added when they are not enough or when the person's risk calls for it. For high blood pressure, guidance lists reduced salt, a diet rich in vegetables and fruit, regular aerobic activity, weight reduction, limited alcohol, and not smoking as the shared base. Medication is then chosen according to cardiovascular risk, not the blood pressure number alone (Hypertension guidance, \"When treatment starts and which options are first-line\"). For type 2 diabetes, structured lifestyle change is paired with medication, and the medication choice depends on other conditions a person has (Type 2 diabetes reference and first-line therapy guidance).\n\nFor some conditions the first-line approach is not a medication. For chronic insomnia, the guidance I found names cognitive behavioral therapy as the first-line treatment (Insomnia guidance). For some infections, such as upper respiratory infections, the guidance focuses on when antibiotics are not needed at all (Antibiotic stewardship guidance).\n\nI can't say whether medication fits your situation without knowing the condition or problem you have in mind. Could you tell me which one? If it is about a specific medicine you were given or considered, that is a question for the prescriber, and I can't advise on starting, stopping, or changing it.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
